<resources>
    <!-- ALIVE palette. The four grounds retired with the flat redesign - the
         page is flat_ground, a face on it is flat_tile, the sentence is flat_ink
         - and they are deleted rather than left as aliases, because a surviving
         alias is how a palette grows a second source of truth. Flat did not
         replace forest, its resting tint, or warm gray, so those stay here. -->
    <color name="alive_forest">#315C45</color>
    <color name="alive_soft_forest">#DCE5DC</color>
    <color name="alive_warm_gray">#D8D2C7</color>

    <!-- Ink with alpha. These are the same ink hue, not new colors, so the
         palette stays six colors while still having a readable hierarchy.
         Warm gray is ~1.2:1 on paper and is never used for text.

         Re-derived from the flat ink #141414: the names survive the ground
         swap because 33 call sites ask for a tone, not for a colour - only the
         hue underneath them moves. -->
    <color name="alive_ink_secondary">#B3141414</color>
    <color name="alive_ink_muted">#99141414</color>
    <color name="alive_ink_disabled">#61141414</color>
    <color name="alive_ink_hairline">#4D141414</color>
    <color name="alive_ink_ripple">#1A141414</color>
    <color name="alive_forest_soft">#33315C45</color>

    <!-- ============ GAME ROLES: three more hues, one job each ============

         Names are roles, not colours, so nobody can spend `gold` on something
         that is not a reward. Each role has three tokens:

           _dark  text and edges. Measured to clear 4.5:1 on every ground in this
                  file, including the other roles' pales.
           _mid   fills only - step blocks, slices, swatches. It never carries
                  text: white on gold_mid is 2.2:1, and ink on red_mid is 4.3:1,
                  so a filled shape is labelled from outside, never on top.
           _pale  a surface to read ink on.

         The gold values are darker than they look on paper. The first draft
         (#8A6414) failed 4.5:1 on warm gray and on all three pales, which the
         palette test now refuses to let back in. -->
    <color name="alive_gold_reward_dark">#6F4E0C</color>
    <color name="alive_gold_reward_mid">#E0A526</color>
    <color name="alive_gold_reward_pale">#F6E7C2</color>

    <!-- Time that is thinning, and the door that closed. Never a large fill:
         a locked day is a pale surface with an ink edge, not a red card. -->
    <color name="alive_red_short_dark">#9E2F22</color>
    <color name="alive_red_short_mid">#D9553F</color>
    <color name="alive_red_short_pale">#F3DAD3</color>

    <!-- The LED trio, never text and never a surface: three tiny status lights
         on the black pill. They are deliberately more saturated than the ink
         roles - a 10dp light on black needs the brightness to read as lit -
         and they keep the red / amber / green identities. -->
    <color name="alive_traffic_red">#FF5F57</color>
    <color name="alive_traffic_yellow">#FEBC2E</color>
    <color name="alive_traffic_green">#28C840</color>

    <!-- The work itself: a Focus session and what it allowed. -->
    <color name="alive_blue_work_dark">#27537A</color>
    <color name="alive_blue_work_mid">#4E86B4</color>
    <!-- Between the mid fill and the pale surface: blue enough to say a card is
         about the day's numbers, light enough that the secondary ink a caption
         is written in still clears 4.5:1 on it. The mid is not: it is a fill and
         never a sentence. -->
    <color name="alive_blue_work_soft">#B9D4E8</color>
    <color name="alive_blue_work_pale">#D9E5EE</color>

    <!-- ============ THE DOING ITSELF: one hue per category ============

         These are not status colours and they are not text colours. A category
         hue lives on exactly two objects - the tab on a quest card's top edge and
         the tile its mark sits in - which is what lets nine of them coexist with
         the role colours above without anyone having to learn a legend.

         Every one is a light: ink strokes and ink text both clear 4.5:1 on all
         nine, because the pixel marks are drawn in ink and sit inside these
         fills. `AlivePaletteTest` measures that. -->
    <color name="alive_cat_study">#F5CE7E</color>
    <color name="alive_cat_reading">#9CC9E8</color>
    <color name="alive_cat_exercise">#63C98A</color>
    <color name="alive_cat_walking">#B7E06B</color>
    <color name="alive_cat_cleaning">#C9CDD2</color>
    <color name="alive_cat_creative">#F58FA6</color>
    <color name="alive_cat_coding">#A99AF2</color>
    <color name="alive_cat_writing">#F4B168</color>
    <!-- Two life missions that had no home among the doing hues: sitting with
         yourself (a muted lavender), and being with people (a sea-glass teal
         that sits in the one wide gap left between exercise green and sky
         blue). Chrome like the rest: a tab and a tile, ink measured on them. -->
    <color name="alive_cat_mindfulness">#C9A3DE</color>
    <color name="alive_cat_social">#77C9B6</color>
    <!-- Focus's WORK activity, and the one category hue that is not a quest
         category. It is a step lighter than blue_work_mid because ink strokes on
         blue_work_mid measure 4.3:1 - a tile whose mark cannot be read is not a
         tile. -->
    <color name="alive_cat_work">#7FA8CB</color>
    <color name="alive_cat_other">#D8D2C7</color>

    <!-- The critter itself: a flat rose body, Clawd-style, with no outline.
         It has to do two jobs alone - hold ink eyes at 4.5:1 and stay visible
         on the paper card it sits on. `AlivePaletteTest` measures both. -->
    <color name="alive_pink_soft">#E58CA4</color>

    <!-- The colour of "do this". Brighter and flatter than reward gold on
         purpose: gold is what a quest paid out, amber is what a button offers,
         and the two are never on the same object. -->
    <color name="alive_amber_action">#F7C948</color>

    <!-- ============ FLAT: the approved language ============

         From FLAT_REDESIGN_SPEC.md sec.03, which takes its numbers from the
         approved `flat-system.css` - the artefact the renders were made with.
         Colour fields are the structure here: a card is a pastel, not a white
         box with an edge, so the field has to carry ink on its own and every
         one of these is measured to do it (`FlatDesignLocksTest`).

         These are added beside the old grounds rather than replacing them in
         one move, so the 151 references can be repointed one build at a time
         and a red suite always names the file that did not move. The old four
         are deleted at the end of the same batch, and `FlatDesignLocksTest`
         fails the build if any of them is still there afterwards. -->
    <color name="flat_ground">#FBFAF8</color>
    <color name="flat_tile">#FFFFFF</color>
    <!-- One value, two roles: a tile is a face on the page, paper is what a
         sentence is written in on a dark ground. Aliased rather than repeated,
         so the second cannot drift from the first. -->
    <color name="flat_paper">@color/flat_tile</color>
    <color name="flat_ink">#141414</color>

